        {receipts.map(({ file, note }) => (
          <div class="artifacts__row">
            <dt>{file}</dt>
            <dd>{note}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
      <p class="fine measure-lg receipts-note">
        Where the kit is stricter than the pinned upstream, it says so: missing
        checks never count as passing, review evidence must match current bytes
        even where upstream guard policy would keep a stale verdict, and
        artifact presence alone never advances a run. These are recorded as
        deliberate differences in{' '}
        <a href={`${kitRepo}/blob/main/docs/parity.json`} target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-umami-event="aidlc-parity">parity.json</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="review" tone="alt" borderTop borderBottom>
    <div class="container">
      <Eyebrow accent="gold" gap="sm">Review the evidence</Eyebrow>
      <h2 class="display-md measure-lg section-title">Lock in a decision on the documents you actually read.</h2>
      <p class="body measure-lg section-copy">
        When Requirements Analysis stops for its produced-artifact review, the optional{' '}
        <a href="/survey/" data-umami-event="aidlc-review-survey">Survey interface</a> displays the current
        artifact bytes, source references, original deadline and remaining worker budget.
        Inspect the documents, then approve, decline, request changes with a correction note,
        or record that you could not confirm. A changed artifact or expired request is refused.
        Resume the same request through Flow Agents after the decision is recorded.
      </p>
      <Grid min="260" gap="md">
        <Card accent="gold" bar="top" pad="md">
          <h3 class="feature-title">Browser, CLI and MCP App</h3>
          <p class="feature-body">Survey 8.1.0 supplies the optional browser console and persisted review session. Its MCP server and embedded MCP App can record decisions against that same session; the host applies them through the browser console. Questionnaire answers and other stages keep their existing typed decision interfaces.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card accent="cobalt-2" bar="top" pad="md">
          <h3 class="feature-title">Shared observations, domain checks</h3>
          <p class="feature-body">The kit retains AWS’s sensor names for methodology-specific checks such as required sections and traceability. Plumb carries bounded check results and their evidence basis. <a href="/veritas/">Veritas</a> consumes the same observation contract for repository standards and readiness; it remains a separate Governance Kit on Flow Agents.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card accent="chalk-2" bar="top" pad="md">
          <h3 class="feature-title">A recorded decision has a scope</h3>
          <p class="feature-body">The local interface records a host-observed operator interaction. A stored name, browser click or MCP call does not authenticate a human. Could not confirm leaves approval unresolved, and requesting changes requires a new author and distinct review within the original budget.</p>
        </Card>
      </Grid>
      <Terminal title="Current kit · command reference, not captured output">
        <pre class="code">{`npm install --save-exact "github:kontourai/aidlc-kit#${currentKitCommit}"
npm install --save-exact @kontourai/survey@8.1.0
npx kontour-aidlc review --controller-root /private/run-controller \\
  --pending /private/run-controller/authority/pending/CURRENT.json \\
  --reference "operator:requirements-reviewed"
# Open the returned loopback URL, inspect the cards, and send the review.
# Resume with the original kit run request and controller.`}</pre>
      </Terminal>
      <p class="term-note">This command reference targets the <a href={`${kitRepo}/tree/${currentKitCommit}`}>current source at {currentKitCommit.slice(0, 7)}</a>. See the <a href={`${kitDocs}docs/using-the-kit.html#optional-survey-requirements-decisions`} data-umami-event="aidlc-review-guide">review and resume guide</a> for setup and the MCP command. Keep the bootstrap and private controller outside the application workspace.</p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section size="lg">
    <div class="container">
      <Eyebrow accent="gold" gap="sm">Status</Eyebrow>
      <h2 class="display-md measure-lg section-title">Early, and specific about what is proven.</h2>
      <Grid min="260" gap="md">
        <Card accent="gold" bar="top" pad="md">
          <h3 class="feature-title">Version 0.1.0, installed from Git</h3>
          <p class="feature-body">The kit is not on npm; <code>@kontourai/aidlc-kit</code> returns 404 from the registry. Install it from GitHub at a commit you have reviewed. The current kit pins Flow 5.2.0 and Flow Agents 8.0.1, and uses Plumb 0.2.0 for shared check observations. The terminal captures below retain their original revision and dependency versions.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card accent="cobalt-2" bar="top" pad="md">
          <h3 class="feature-title">Method coverage, not runtime equivalence</h3>
          <p class="feature-body">Stage selection, order, roles, artifact contracts and policies are compared against the pinned source. That does not show the kit behaves like the upstream engine.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card accent="red-2" bar="top" pad="md">
          <h3 class="feature-title">Effectiveness not verified</h3>
          <p class="feature-body">Independent effectiveness remains withheld pending final-source qualification and grading. The parity ledger records effectiveness as <code>not_verified</code>, and nothing here claims the kit produces better software.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card accent="chalk-2" bar="top" pad="md">
          <h3 class="feature-title">Operations need your providers</h3>
          <p class="feature-body">Deployment and operation stages need a host-supplied provider and existing authorization. Selecting a stage never deploys anything on its own. Live runs need Node 24, Docker and model credentials.</p>
        </Card>
      </Grid>
    </div>
  </Section>

